9	 *    unwrapping could collide with the enclosing scope. When the consequent
10	 *    does NOT terminate, the branches must become two separate ifs
11	 *    (`if (x)` / `if (!x)`); that rewrite is left to a human, because the
12	 *    consequent may have changed the state the negated condition would read.
13	 *
14	 * 2. A terminating `if` (no else) whose body outweighs the statements after
15	 *    it — the whole happy path wrapped in `if (x) {...}` with a dangling
16	 *    fallback tail. Flip it: `if (!x)` guards the tail, the happy path goes
17	 *    flat. Detect-only; moving blocks across comments is where codemods eat
18	 *    code.
